A||||6||JohnnyK||Signs of clutch going.||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||07-25-2000||02:56 AM||||I have a car with 138000 Miles on it, and I don't know the history. How can I tell if the clutch it going to go? I know there will be slip, but how long before it is not driveable? Will I have lots of warning, or will it do it in the course of an hour? Also, (it's a 2.5), in first, if I punch the throttle while in the low rpm range, the car WILL kick forward. Does this mean the clutch isn't slipping yet?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gokart||07-25-2000||06:01 AM||||Good question Johnny, wish I had the answer but I'd like to know myself. Mine's getting softer on me and the pedal never went all the way to the floor before but does now. It's got plenty of fluid but maybe needs to be bled? Clutch experts...little help for us <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||5
Z||000002||batboy||07-25-2000||11:14 AM||||Johnny, It's very obvious when the clutch disc starts wearing out, it'll begin slipping and you'll know it. Once they start slipping bad, they go out pretty quick.<P>Gokart, your problem sounds more hydraulic related and very similar to what I'm going through with my 85 GT right now. After much research and troubleshooting, I'm hoping my problem is a bad master cylinder. Just got a new one today, but it'll be a few days before I get it put in. I'll let you know if it fixes my problem. Check out this article.<P> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||07-25-2000||11:38 AM||||Typically,<P>A wearing clutch will begin slipping in higher gears first, and then progress down(5th, then 4th, then 3rd...). If you are in say 4th or 5th gear, with the engine running in the power band and clutch fully enguaged, and you kick the gas pedal down real, if the rpms go up and the speedo stays just about the same, then you have a clutch slipping. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||07-25-2000||07:05 PM||||Good link Batboy, I forgot about Ed Parks' site.  <BR>One question, though.  I always thought you had to have the flywheel ground when doing a clutch.  Dealer wants $268 for a new (86 2.8) flywheel, and $1.60 apiece for the flywheel to crank bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||07-26-2000||07:09 PM||||Yes, with non-hydraulic clutches, having the flywheel turned was standard proceedure. They do make shims to put between the crank and flywheel to make up the diff in thickness. What Ed is saying, if the flywheel looks good and is not grooved, then just use it. If you turn it down without the shim, you'll probably have early clutch failure.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||07-27-2000||10:27 PM||||That could explain why mine is starting to slip so early.  When I did it the last time, it was blued, but not scored.  <BR>Ram says the warranty is invalid unless I have it ground, if I use their Powergrip (Kevlar on one side of the disk, and "30% more holding power").  Since their OEM replacement isn't faring so well, I may switch to this one.  They may honor the warranty, but I have to send the old one to them so they can validate it... ||||reg||1
